  "narrative": {
    "northStar": "The Reading Room",
    "overview": "Omnibus is a quiet, lamp-lit library. The grounds are warm near-blacks, every surface is a slightly lighter tone of the same warm neutral, and the only strong color in the chrome is a single lamplight amber. The books bring everything else: covers are the most colorful, most lifted objects on any screen, and each book can tint its own surroundings with its `accent_color`.\n\nThe voice is editorial and calm. Titles are set in Cormorant Garamond, the interface in Instrument Sans, and labels and numbers in Space Mono uppercase, like a catalogue card. Calm is not static, though: Omnibus likes motion and interactivity. Covers rise under the pointer, sheets slide in, and loaders breathe. The chrome stays quiet until it is touched, then responds.\n\nThe same tokens drive both web clients: the Dioxus app (`frontend/assets/atrium.css`) and the React client (`frontend-js/src/styles/theme.css`). Four themes swap the token values through `data-theme`: dark (the default, documented in the frontmatter), black, light and sepia.",
    "keyCharacteristics": [
      "Warm oklch neutrals (hue 70–80, chroma near zero) in four tonal steps, with no pure greys outside the black theme.",
      "One chrome accent, Lamplight Amber, used sparingly; covers carry their own color.",
      "A serif voice for titles, sans for interface, uppercase mono for labels.",
      "Layered depth: tonal steps first, then soft shadows, from cards up to covers and floating layers.",
      "Responsive motion that respects `prefers-reduced-motion`."
    ],
    "rules": [
      {
        "name": "The Covers Bring the Color Rule",
        "body": "Chrome stays in the warm neutrals plus one amber. Any other hue on a screen should come from a book: a cover, its per-book `--accent`, or a highlight color (amber, green, blue, rose and violet, for the reader's highlights only).",
        "section": "colors"
      },
      {
        "name": "The Neutral Plate Rule",
        "body": "A book without a cover gets a neutral plate (`cover-fallback-bg` / `cover-fallback-ink`), never the accent, so a missing cover never looks like a selected one.",
        "section": "colors"
      },
      {
        "name": "The Lining Figures Rule",
        "body": "Numbers are data: `font-variant-numeric: lining-nums` is set app-wide, because Cormorant's old-style zero reads as a lowercase \"o\".",
        "section": "typography"
      },
      {
        "name": "The Upright Emphasis Rule",
        "body": "`<em>` inside a heading stays upright. Emphasis in a title comes from weight or color, not italics.",
        "section": "typography"
      },
      {
        "name": "The Distance Rule",
        "body": "A shadow's size says how far the object sits from the page, from card to cover to popover to modal to sheet. Never give a lower layer a bigger shadow than a layer above it.",
        "section": "elevation"
      },
      {
        "name": "The Frosted Chrome Rule",
        "body": "Fixed chrome is translucent: the top bar is `bg-0` at 92% with a 10px blur, and the tab bar is `bg-1` at 92% with a 12px blur.",
        "section": "elevation"
      }
    ],
    "dos": [
      "**Do** let books carry the color: the cover art, the per-book `--accent`, highlight colors.",
      "**Do** animate in response to people: a cover lifts, a sheet opens over 240ms (`--ease-out`, `cubic-bezier(.16,1,.3,1)`) and closes over 160ms (`--ease-in`), hovers take 0.15s.",
      "**Do** make surfaces respond to the reader rather than sit still; interactivity is part of the calm, not at odds with it.",
      "**Do** wrap every animation in `prefers-reduced-motion`; under `reduce`, loaders fade instead of moving and entrance animations don't run.",
      "**Do** draw keyboard focus as `outline: 2px solid var(--accent)` with a 2px offset on every interactive element, including buttons and chips.",
      "**Do** put labels and numbers in Space Mono uppercase, and titles in Cormorant.",
      "**Do** check every new color against all four themes (dark, black, light, sepia)."
